1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:18,240
As the latest crop of students pen their undergraduate application form and weigh up their options, it may be worth considering just how the point, purpose and value of a degree have changed and what Generation Z need to consider as they start the third stage of their educational journey.
在新一批学生填写本科申请表、权衡各种选择之际，或许值得思考一下：学位的意义、目的和价值究竟发生了怎样的变化，Z世代在开启求学之路的第三阶段时又需要考虑些什么。

2
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:25,120
Millennials were told that if you did well in school and got a decent degree, you would be set up for life.
千禧一代听到的说法是：只要在学校表现出色、拿到一个像样的学位，这辈子就有了保障。

3
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:28,160
But that promise has been found wanting.
但事实证明，这一承诺靠不住。

4
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:32,470
As degrees became universal, they became devalued.
随着学位变得普及，学位也就贬值了。

5
00:00:33,270 --> 00:00:36,730
Education was no longer a secure route to social mobility.
教育不再是实现社会阶层流动的可靠途径。

6
00:00:37,530 --> 00:00:46,510
Today, 28 percent of graduates in the UK are in non-graduate roles, a percentage which is double the average among OECD countries.
如今，英国有28%的大学毕业生在不需要大学学历的岗位上工作，这一比例是经合组织国家平均水平的两倍。

7
00:00:47,310 --> 00:01:00,340
This is not to say that there is no point in getting a degree, but rather to stress that a degree is not for everyone, that the switch from classroom to lecture hall is not an inevitable one and that other options are available.
这并不是说读学位毫无意义，而是要强调：学位并非适合所有人，从教室到大学讲堂的转变并不是必然的，而且还有其他选择。

8
00:01:01,140 --> 00:01:12,840
Thankfully, there are signs that this is already happening, with Generation Z seeking to learn from their millennial predecessors, even if parents and teachers tend to be still set in the degree mindset.
值得庆幸的是，已经有迹象表明这种情况正在发生：Z世代正设法从前辈千禧一代身上汲取经验，即便家长和老师往往仍固守着看重学位的思维定式。

9
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:22,530
Employers have long seen the advantages of hiring school leavers who often prove themselves to be more committed and loyal employees than graduates.
雇主们早就看到了雇用中学毕业生的好处：比起大学毕业生，他们往往证明自己是更敬业、更忠诚的员工。

10
00:01:23,330 --> 00:01:28,160
Many too are seeing the advantages of scrapping a degree requirement for certain roles.
许多雇主也逐渐看到了对某些岗位取消学位要求的好处。

11
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:34,750
For those for whom a degree is the desired route, consider that this may well be the first of many.
对于那些想走学位这条路的人来说，要考虑到：这很可能是众多学位中的第一个。

12
00:01:35,550 --> 00:01:39,980
In this age of generalists, it pays to have specific knowledge or skills.
在这个通才遍地的时代，拥有专门的知识或技能会带来回报。

13
00:01:40,780 --> 00:01:44,190
Postgraduates now earn 40 percent more than graduates.
如今，研究生的收入比本科毕业生高40%。

14
00:01:44,990 --> 00:01:48,640
When more and more of us have a degree, it makes sense to have two.
我们当中有学位的人越来越多，拥有两个学位也就合情合理了。

15
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:59,480
It is unlikely that Generation Z will be done with education at 18 or 21: they will need to be constantly upskilling throughout their career to stay employable.
Z世代不太可能在18岁或21岁就与教育告别：为了保持就业能力，他们需要在整个职业生涯中不断提升技能。

16
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:15,010
It has been estimated that this generation, due to the pressures of technology, the wish for personal fulfillment and desire for diversity, will work for 17 different employers over the course of their working life and have five different careers.
据估计，由于技术带来的压力、对个人成就感的向往以及对多样化的渴望，这一代人在整个工作生涯中将为17个不同的雇主工作，并从事五种不同的职业。

17
00:02:15,810 --> 00:02:22,280
Education, and not just knowledge gained on campus, will be a core part of Generation Z’s career trajectory.
教育，而不仅仅是在校园里获得的知识，将成为Z世代职业轨迹的核心组成部分。

18
00:02:23,080 --> 00:02:30,550
Older generations often talk about their degree in the present and personal tense: ‘I am a geographer’ or ‘I am a classicist’.
老一辈人谈起自己的学位时，常常用现在时、以第一人称来表述：“我是地理学家”或“我是古典学者”。

19
00:02:31,350 --> 00:02:38,600
Their sons or daughters would never say such a thing; it’s as if they already know that their degree won’t define them in the same way.
他们的子女绝不会说这种话，就好像他们早已明白，学位不会以同样的方式来定义自己。
